{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And the children of Israel did as HaShem commanded Moses</b> — for semikhah (laying hands), for dikhah (pushing), for teliyah (hanging), for \"you shall not let his corpse remain on the tree\" — Torat Kohanim (chapter 20, mishnah 10). And R. Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Leviticus 24:23) said in its plain sense, that they did from that day onward according to this law for one who wounds. But it is not correct that it should be written about the future. And its plain sense is \"and they stoned him with a stone\" — that the children of Israel performed the commandment that HaShem commanded Moses, for thus Scripture repeats concerning all who observe the commandments of HaShem, as it says in the pericope of the Paschal offering (Exodus 12:28), and as it says in the pericope of the census: \"And the children of Israel did according to all that HaShem commanded Moses, so they did\" (Numbers 1:54), and so in the pericope of the tribes of the princes: \"And Moses did as HaShem commanded him, so he did\" (ibid. 17:26). And what seems correct in my eyes here is: since at the beginning he specified the stoning and afterward returned and said \"and the children of Israel did,\" the reason Scripture says this is that when Moses spoke to the children of Israel, they took the blasphemer out and stoned him, and all the children of Israel did so, to observe and to do as HaShem commanded Moses — not out of hatred for the Egyptian's son who quarreled with the Israelite, but to eradicate the blemish from among them."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:59:15.903369694Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}